        <div class="mcp-faq-list">
          <details class="glass mcp-faq-item" open>
            <summary class="mcp-faq-item__q">
              <span>Which plan is required for the Model Context Protocol (MCP) server?</span>
              <span class="mcp-faq-arrow">&darr;</span>
            </summary>
            <div class="mcp-faq-item__a">
              <p>
                Access to the 10zebra MCP server requires an active <strong>Director plan</strong> ($79/mo) or higher.
              </p>
              <p>
                Because autonomous LLM agents can dispatch parallel renders and restructure multi-scene projects in seconds, the Director plan includes dedicated high-throughput cloud queues, 3,500 monthly credits, 30% discount on credit top-ups, and project-isolated budget controls so your agents never exceed their authorized spend.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="glass mcp-faq-item">
            <summary class="mcp-faq-item__q">
              <span>What is an MCP server and how does 10zebra implement it?</span>
              <span class="mcp-faq-arrow">&darr;</span>
            </summary>
            <div class="mcp-faq-item__a">
              <p>
                An <strong>MCP (Model Context Protocol) server</strong> is an open-standard gateway created by Anthropic that connects AI reasoning assistants (like Claude, Cursor, and custom agent frameworks) to external application systems.
              </p>
              <p>
                The 10zebra MCP server exposes our multi-model AI video production platform as {stats.totalTools} callable tools, {stats.totalResources} live data resources, and {stats.totalPrompts} pre-built directorial prompts. This allows your LLM assistant to act as a full-fledged video producer: inspecting scenes, writing prompts, triggering generations across models like Kling 3.0 and Google Veo 3, and trimming the sequence directly from chat.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="glass mcp-faq-item">
            <summary class="mcp-faq-item__q">
              <span>How does 10zebra prevent AI agents from overspending or draining my wallet?</span>
              <span class="mcp-faq-arrow">&darr;</span>
            </summary>
            <div class="mcp-faq-item__a">
              <p>
                Unlike generic API keys that connect directly to personal credit card lines, 10zebra enforces spend limits server-side inside <strong>atomic Firestore transactions</strong> on dedicated project-level ledgers (<code>users/&#123;uid&#125;/stories/&#123;story_id&#125;/credits/0</code>).
              </p>
              <p>
                An agent has no direct wallet access. If a project runs out of allocated credits, the generation tool immediately returns an <strong>HTTP 402 Payment Required</strong> error with a direct browser funding URL (<code>https://10zebra.com/p/&#123;portal_id&#125;</code>). This prevents runaway spending while informing the user exactly how to authorize further production.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="glass mcp-faq-item">
            <summary class="mcp-faq-item__q">
              <span>Can an agent use different AI video models for different shots in one video?</span>
              <span class="mcp-faq-arrow">&darr;</span>
            </summary>
            <div class="mcp-faq-item__a">
              <p>
                Yes. 10zebra is designed from the ground up for multi-model storytelling. Your agent can read the <code>models://by-method</code> resource to inspect active models, and choose different engines for different shots: for instance, using <strong>Google Veo 3</strong> for cinematic panoramic landscapes, <strong>Kling 3.0</strong> for complex character movements, and <strong>Runway Gen-4</strong> for stylistic transitions.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="glass mcp-faq-item">
            <summary class="mcp-faq-item__q">
              <span>How do I update the documentation when the app codebase changes?</span>
              <span class="mcp-faq-arrow">&darr;</span>
            </summary>
            <div class="mcp-faq-item__a">
              <p>
                10zebra provides a unified update script: <code>npm run sync:app</code> (or <code>npm run sync:mcp</code>). This parses the backend <code>mcp/server.py</code> and <code>functions/mcp.js</code> files, automatically re-indexing all tool signatures, parameter tables, resources, and model rates across the site.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>
